.TH WAYHUD 5 "2026-09-01" "wayhud" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
wayhud \- configuration file for wayhud(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wayhud/config.toml
.PP
.RI ( ~/.config/wayhud/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, empty, or not an absolute path.)
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file is TOML. It holds one table,
.BR style ,
whose sub\-tables are named presets; a preset is chosen at run time with
.BR "wayhud \-\-style " \fINAME\fR
and defaults to
.BR default .
.PP
A missing file is not an error \- every preset then consists entirely of the
built\-in defaults listed below. A file that fails to parse
.I is
an error, as is a key the program does not recognise: a misspelt setting is
reported rather than silently ignored.
.PP
.B [style.default]
is the base for every other preset. A key a preset does not set is taken from
there, and only then from the built\-in default listed below; so the shared
font and colours are written once rather than copied into every block.
.PP
Sub\-tables
.RB ( reveal ,
.BR vanish ,
.BR sound )
merge key by key, so a preset can change one knob and keep the rest of the
base's. The exception is a
.B kind
that differs from the base's: that replaces the sub\-table outright, because
the remaining keys would belong to the other variant.
.PP
A key set in
.B [style.default]
therefore applies everywhere unless a preset overrides it \- including back to
a different value.
.PP
Command\-line flags override the selected preset for that invocation; see
.BR wayhud (1).
.SH VALUE TYPES
.TP
.I colour
Any colour GTK's CSS parser accepts:
.BR #b8bb26 ,
.BR #b8bb26cc ,
.BR "rgba(184,187,38,0.8)" ,
or a named colour such as
.BR green .
.TP
.I font
A Pango font description \- family, optional style words, then size in
points, e.g.
.B "\(dqMonospace 72\(dq"
or
.BR "\(dqFiraCode Nerd Font 72\(dq" .
Note that the message itself is plain text: Pango markup in it is not
interpreted.
.IP
.BR Monospace ,
.B Sans
and
.B Serif
are fontconfig generics: they resolve to whatever the system has configured
for that role, so they work on any machine. A real family name must be spelled
the way fontconfig spells it, which is rarely how the vendor writes it \-
.B "\(dqFiraCode Nerd Font\(dq"
resolves,
.B "\(dqFira Code\(dq"
does not. A name that does not resolve falls back to the system default
without a word of warning, which for a HUD usually means a proportional face
where a fixed\-width one was meant. Check one with
.BR fc\-match (1).
.TP
.I pixels
Logical pixels, i.e. before the output's scale factor is applied. On a
.B scale 2
display a value of 64 covers 128 device pixels.
.SH KEYS
All keys below live directly in a
.BI [style. NAME ]
table.
.SS Text
.TP
.B font
.RI ( font ,
default
.BR "\(dqMonospace 72\(dq" )
.br
Typeface and size of the message. The default is the fontconfig generic
rather than a named family, so an unconfigured install renders in the
system's monospaced font instead of silently falling back.
.TP
.B color
.RI ( colour ,
default
.BR \(dq#b8bb26\(dq )
.br
Fill colour of the glyphs.
.TP
.B outline
.RI ( colour ,
default
.BR \(dq#1d2021\(dq )
.br
Colour of the stroke around each glyph. Omit the key, or set it to
.BR \(dqnone\(dq ,
to draw the text flat with no outline pass. A preset inheriting an outline
from
.B [style.default]
needs the explicit
.B \(dqnone\(dq
\- TOML has no null to remove an inherited key with.
.TP
.B outline_width
.RI ( float ,
.IR pixels ;
default: derived from the font size)
.br
Width of that stroke. Left unset it is the font size divided by 14, which
keeps the visible halo a fixed fraction of the stem thickness across sizes;
a constant that looks like a fine contour at 72pt reads as fake bold at
24pt. Set it to pin an exact width, 0 to 128 \- the same ceiling as
.BR glow.radius ,
because both are added to the padding and the padding is subtracted from the
monitor width that decides where lines wrap.
.TP
.B glow
.RI ( table ,
default: absent)
.br
A soft halo behind the glyphs:
.BR "{ color = " \fICOLOUR\fR ", radius = " \fIF\fR ", alpha = " \fIF\fR " }" .
Omit the key for no halo.
.RS
.TP
.B color
.RI ( colour ,
default
.BR \(dq#b8bb26\(dq )
The halo's colour. Matching the fill reads as the glyph emitting light, which
is the point; a contrasting one reads as a printing error.
.TP
.B radius
(float, 0\(en128
.IR pixels ;
default
.BR 12.0 )
Blur radius.
.B 0
is off \- which is how a preset takes back a halo inherited from
.BR [style.default] ,
TOML having no null, the same reason
.B outline
needs the literal
.BR \(dqnone\(dq .
The range is enforced because this figure widens the padding, and the padding
is subtracted from the monitor width to get the wrapping budget.
.TP
.B alpha
(float, 0\(en1; default
.BR 0.55 )
Peak opacity of the halo where it leaves the glyph.
.RE
.IP
The halo is painted
.I under
the outline rather than instead of it, so the two combine. It is blurred once
per output when the message is built, not per frame.
.TP
.B line_align
.RB ( left " | " center " | " right ,
default
.BR left )
.br
How lines are aligned relative to each other inside the text block. This is
independent of where the block itself sits.
.SS Placement
.TP
.B halign
.RB ( left " | " center " | " right ,
default
.BR center )
.br
Horizontal placement of the text block on the output. The names are physical
edges, not the
.BR start / end
of CSS and GTK: the surface is anchored to a real edge of the monitor, so
.B left
stays the left edge whatever the text direction. Each axis takes only its own
names \-
.B "halign = \(dqtop\(dq"
is an error, not a puzzle.
.TP
.B valign
.RB ( top " | " center " | " bottom ,
default
.BR center )
.br
Vertical placement.
.TP
.B margin
.RI ( integer ,
.IR pixels ;
default
.BR 64 )
.br
Gap between the text block and the edge it is anchored to. An axis set to
.B center
is centred by the compositor and ignores this value.
.SS Timing
.TP
.B timeout_ms
.RI ( integer ,
milliseconds; default
.BR 5000 )
.br
How long the fully revealed text stays up. Measured from the
.I end
of the reveal, not from start\-up, so a slow typewriter does not eat into
the reading time.
.IP
The whole lifetime \- reveal plus hold plus vanish \- is capped at 3600000 ms
(one hour), and the message is refused if it would exceed that: there is no
way to dismiss a HUD early, so an accidental extra digit, a tiny
.B cps
or a huge vanish
.B ms
would all strand it on screen.
.TP
.B reveal
.RI ( table ,
default
.BR "{ kind = \(dqtypewriter\(dq, cps = 28, cursor = true }" )
.br
How the text appears. See
.B REVEAL
below.
.TP
.B vanish
.RI ( table ,
default
.BR "{ kind = \(dqcollapse\(dq, ms = 420 }" )
.br
How it goes away. See
.B VANISH
below.
.SS Sound
.TP
.B sound
.RI ( table ,
default
.BR "{ enabled = true, freq = 2100.0, decay_ms = 38.0, gain = 0.22, every = 1 }" )
.br
The typewriter blip. See
.B SOUND
below.
.SH REVEAL
A table with a
.B kind
key selecting the variant.
.TP
.B { kind = "instant" }
The whole message appears at once.
.TP
.BR "{ kind = \(dqtypewriter\(dq, cps = " \fIF\fR ", cursor = " \fIBOOL\fR ", jitter = " \fIF\fR " }"
Typed out character by character.
.RS
.TP
.B cps
(float, default 28) Speed in characters per second. Zero or less is treated
as
.BR instant .
.TP
.B cursor
(boolean, default true) Draw a block caret at the write head. It keeps
blinking while the text is held.
.TP
.B jitter
(float, 0\(en1; default 0) Randomise each gap between keystrokes by up to
this fraction of it, either way: 0 is a metronome, 0.3 varies every gap by up
to 30%. The variation is symmetric, so the reveal still takes about
.I chars
/
.I cps
seconds overall. Values above 1 are rejected, since a gap would have to be
negative.
.IP
The blips follow the same moments as the glyphs \- they are the same numbers,
not a second calculation \- so sound and animation cannot drift apart.
.RE
.SH VANISH
A table with a
.B kind
key. Every variant except
.B instant
takes
.B ms
(integer, default 420) for its duration.
.TP
.B instant
Gone on the frame the hold expires.
.TP
.B fade
Alpha to zero.
.TP
.B collapse
CRT power\-off: the text squashes towards its middle line, blooms slightly
wider, washes to white and blinks out.
.TP
.B wash
A soft edge sweeps through the text, erasing as it passes. Takes an extra
.B dir
of
.B down
(default) or
.BR up .
.TP
.B untype
The caret walks back and eats the text. The only vanish that makes a sound,
because it is typing; it gets a caret even when the reveal was instant.
.TP
.B dissolve
The glyphs fall apart into blocks, in an order that is pseudo\-random but
fixed, so the decay is stable rather than flickering.
.SH SOUND
The blip is synthesised at run time rather than loaded from a file. The knob
names match
.BR blyamk (1),
so a sound dialled in there with
.B blyamk \-v
transfers over verbatim.
.TP
.B enabled
(boolean, default
.BR true )
.br
Whether to make any noise at all.
.B \-\-no\-sound
overrides this to false.
.TP
.B freq
(float, 100\(en8000 Hz; default
.BR 2100.0 )
.br
Base frequency of the blip \- its overall pitch.
.TP
.B decay_ms
(float, 10\(en3000 milliseconds; default
.BR 38.0 )
.br
Ring\-out time. Longer values turn a click into a tone. The range is
enforced because this figure sizes the synthesis buffer.
.TP
.B gain
(float, 0\(en1; default
.BR 0.22 )
.br
Peak amplitude of a single blip. Overlapping blips sum, and the mix is
clamped rather than limited, so a high gain at a high
.B cps
will clip.
.TP
.B every
(integer, at least 1; default
.BR 1 )
.br
Blip once every N revealed characters. Whitespace never blips \- a space bar
on a movie terminal does not click, and newlines would stutter.
.SH EXAMPLE
Only
.B [style.default]
spells out the common settings; the others state their differences.
.PP
A complete file with every key at its default is shipped as
.I config.example.toml
in the source distribution.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[style.default]
font = "FiraCode Nerd Font 72"   # a real family; must match fontconfig
color = "#b8bb26"
outline = "#1d2021"
halign = "center"
valign = "center"
timeout_ms = 5000
reveal = { kind = "typewriter", cps = 28, cursor = true }
vanish = { kind = "collapse", ms = 420 }

# Inherits font, outline and placement from default.
[style.alert]
color = "#fb4934"
timeout_ms = 8000
vanish = { kind = "wash", ms = 300, dir = "up" }

# Self\-destructing note: types in, sits, clicks its way back out.
[style.spy]
color = "#8ec07c"
reveal = { kind = "typewriter", cps = 18, cursor = true }
vanish = { kind = "untype", ms = 900 }

# Small, cornered, silent \- for something that just needs to be on screen.
[style.corner]
font = "Monospace 24"
halign = "right"
valign = "bottom"
margin = 32
reveal = { kind = "instant" }
vanish = { kind = "fade", ms = 200 }
sound = { enabled = false }
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wayhud (1)
